09. ORGANIC TERRAIN AND PLATFORM REDESIGN
============================================================

The existing gameplay terrain uses reliable but visually
simple rectangular collision platforms.

Preserve their functionality.

However, their visible appearance must be upgraded.

The current visual language feels too repetitive and
geometrically uniform.

We want the platforms to look like natural formations
that belong to the Veilgrove.

Create an organic terrain art kit.

A. ANCIENT ROOT PLATFORMS

- Uneven moss-covered edges.
- Layered bark.
- Twisting root strands.
- Hanging roots beneath platforms.
- Small vegetation clusters.
- Organic top surfaces.
- Natural variations in thickness.

B. STONE FORMATIONS

- Irregular silhouettes.
- Multiple rock layers.
- Moss-covered surfaces.
- Small plants growing through cracks.
- Natural color variation.
- Visible material depth.

C. LIVING PLATFORMS

- Giant flowers.
- Large organic petals.
- Glowing veins.
- Rooted growth structures.
- Animated plant surfaces.
- Integrated environmental illumination.

D. HAZARDS

- Natural irregular thorn formations.
- Thorn clusters growing out of roots and soil.
- Organic hazard silhouettes.
- Clear warning colors.
- Appropriate animation and visual telegraphs.

IMPORTANT VISUAL ASSEMBLY RULE:

Do not present the visible terrain as a plain rectangle
with a decorative line at its top.

Create a combination of different visual elements:

1. Organic top silhouette.
2. Moss or grass surface.
3. Main bark or rock body.
4. Side texture.
5. Hanging roots.
6. Small plants.
7. Vegetation overlays.
8. Environmental details.

Use deliberate overlap between assets.

Allow decorative roots and vegetation to extend beyond
the basic collision shape.

Vary the platform top silhouettes.

Create different visual formations for different sections.

Make the terrain appear grown into the world rather than
placed into it.

NEVER compromise collision readability.

The visible artwork and physics bodies must remain separate.

Players must immediately understand which surfaces
are walkable and where hazards begin.

